he largest powers for $u$ and $(v-2)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For ${u}^{3}$ and $u,$ the largest power is $3.$\n\n- For $(v-2)^{2}$ and $(v-2)^{5},$ the largest power is $5.$\n\nNow, we raise $u$ and $(v-2)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}({u}^{3}(v-2)^{2},\\,u(v-2)^{5})={u}^{3}\\cdot (v-2)^{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(4{u}^{3}(v-2)^{2},\\,5u(v-2)^{5})=20{u}^{3}(v-2)^{5}\n$$" 13194139533702 true] [17592186247605 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247606 13194139533702 true] [17592186247605 1089 "q-111582" 13194139533702 true] [17592186247605 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533702 true] [17592186247605 1100 "Find the least common multiple of $2(z-4)^{2}$ and $7(z-4)^{2}.$" 13194139533702 true] [17592186247605 1113 #uuid "a9c887ca-8741-5266-8dc6-cbf04d972b43" 13194139533702 true] [17592186247605 1138 false 13194139533702 true] [17592186247605 1145 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(2,7):$\n\n- Multiples of $2:$ $\\,2,\\,4,…,\\,12,\\,14,16,…$\n\n- Multiples of $7:$ $\\,7,\\,14,\\,21,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(2,7)=14.$\n\nSince the variable part is the same in the expressions, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((z-4)^{2},\\,(z-4)^{2})=(z-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2(z-4)^{2},\\,7(z-4)^{2})=14\\cdot (z-4)^{2}=14(z-4)^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533702 true] [17592186247606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247607 13194139533702 true] [17592186247606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247608 13194139533702 true] [17592186247606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247609 13194139533702 true] [17592186247606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247610 13194139533702 true] [17592186247606 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247611 13194139533702 true] [175921