common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(8,20):$\n\n- Multiples of $8:$ $\\,8,\\,16,\\,24,\\,32,\\,40,…$\n\n- Multiples of $20:$ $\\,20,\\,40,\\,60,\\,80,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(8,20)=40.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(y-2)^{9}$ and $(y-2)^{7},$ we raise $(y-2)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((y-2)^{9},\\,(y-2)^{7})=(y-2)^{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(8(y-2)^{9},\\,20(y-2)^{7})=40\\cdot (y-2)^{9}=40(y-2)^{9}\n$$" 13194139533702 true] [17592186247639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247640 13194139533702 true] [17592186247639 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247641 13194139533702 true] [17592186247639 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533702 true] [17592186247639 1041 "field-1" 13194139533702 true] [17592186247639 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247640 13194139533702 true] [17592186247639 1110 #uuid "ebacfeb4-97e9-57a9-9e2b-bf51c61b29b8" 13194139533702 true] [17592186247640 1034 "40(y-2)^{9}" 13194139533702 true] [17592186247640 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533702 true] [17592186247640 1111 #uuid "e8fe8b0b-6594-5981-bbac-175daf88f547" 13194139533702 true] [17592186247641 1034 "40(y-2)^{7}" 13194139533702 true] [17592186247641 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533702 true] [17592186247641 1111 #uuid "8f8a9341-7658-542e-9d15-a6a0f8c4372f" 13194139533702 true] [17592186247642 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247643 13194139533702 true] [17592186247642 1089 "q-222107" 13194139533702 true] [17592186247642 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533702 true] [17592186247642 1100 "The least common multiple of the polynomials $3(x-3)^{3}(y+1)$ and $5(x-3)(y+1)^{2}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533702 true] [17592186247642 1113 #uuid "5df2a3d2-5afd-5ea4-84e8-b1